As a result of mastering the discipline, the student:
1. must know:
major theories and perspectives of human development;
research methods used in developmental psychology;
biological and environmental influences on development;
major physical, cognitive, emotional, and social changes across the lifespan;
key developmental characteristics of infancy, childhood, adolescence, adulthood, and late adulthood.
2. must be able to:
apply developmental psychology concepts to practical cases and real-life situations;
compare and critically evaluate major developmental theories;
interpret developmental research and identify appropriate research methods;
analyze the interaction of biological, psychological, social, and environmental factors in development;
formulate evidence-based explanations of developmental processes across the lifespan.
3. must own:
basic methods of analyzing developmental research and case material;
skills of academic discussion, critical analysis, and application of developmental concepts.

